        <item>
            <title>0.18.0</title>
            <pubDate>Mon, 21 Sep 2026 20:04:40 +0200</pubDate>
            <sparkle:version>1800</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>0.18.0</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>14.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <sparkle:hardwareRequirements>arm64</sparkle:hardwareRequirements>
            <description><![CDATA[<h2>Jarvis 0.18.0</h2>
<p><b>Tell Jarvis what else counts as a task.</b> Jarvis already catches the
things people ask you for and the things you promise. Now you can write, in
your own words, anything else it should treat as yours — and it starts
catching those too.</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Write it in Settings → Focus.</b> A new "What else counts as a task"
      section, with one input that grows as you type — a rule can be a
      sentence or a paragraph. ⌘↵ adds it. Your rules are listed above it, and
      an ✕ removes one whenever it stops being true.</li>
  <li><b>It works on mail you already have.</b> Adding a rule re-reads the
      last week, so it proves itself straight away instead of waiting for the
      next email to arrive.</li>
  <li><b>The tasks it finds link to the thing to open.</b> When the email
      names a ticket, a document or a review, that link sits on the task's
      evidence — labelled with the site it goes to, so you can see where it
      leads before you click.</li>
  <li><b>Jarvis reads notification mail properly now.</b> Deploy notices,
      billing mail and anything else wrapped in machine formatting used to be
      skipped whole, and any real task inside them went with it. Jarvis now
      reads past the noise and keeps the message.</li>
  <li><b>Smaller switches in Settings</b>, drawn in Jarvis's own colour rather
      than the system's.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
